    "source_evidence": [
      {
        "quote": "However, the inverse association between serum adiponectin levels and incidence of metabolic syndrome was observed in premenopausal women with obesity (OR 0.16, 95% CI 0.03-0.81), but not in postmenopausal women with obesity (OR 0.55, 95% CI 0.27-1.14).",
        "sentence_id": 8,
        "side": "a"
      },
      {
        "quote": "On multivariate logistic regression analysis, adiponectin (p = 0.020), HOMA-IR, age (p = 0.011) and BMI (p = 0.019) were independently associated with the metabolic syndrome, but not FAI (p = 0.256).",
        "sentence_id": 8,
        "side": "b"
      }
    ]
  ,
    "uncertain_extraction": false
  },
  "factor": "unknown",
  "label": "not_comparable",
  "rationale": "Both claims concern serum adiponectin and metabolic syndrome, but the pair is unfit for directional comparison. Side A (prospective cohort, 1,219 women) reports that the inverse association between adiponectin and incident metabolic syndrome over an average 2.5-year follow-up was 'not observed' in postmenopausal women with obesity (OR 0.55, 95% CI 0.27-1.14), an explicit absence of the inverse association in that subgroup. Side B (cross-sectional, 116 Chinese women with PCOS) reports only that adiponectin was 'independently associated with the metabolic syndrome' on multivariate logistic regression (p = 0.020) and never states the direction of the estimate. Because side B's direction is unstated, it cannot be determined whether its association is inverse (conflicting with A's unconfirmed inverse association) or positive (potentially compatible with it). The differing populations (postmenopausal obese women vs women with PCOS) and designs (prospective incidence vs cross-sectional association) are additional source-supported differences, but the decisive obstacle is the missing direction on side B, leaving essential interpretation uncertain."
}
